## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to architect a production-grade, multimodal NLP system that balances real-time performance with privacy requirements while supporting multilingual deployment.

## Key Concepts
- Multimodal fusion strategies and on-device inference
- Privacy-preserving cloud inference with encrypted fallback
- Latency budgeting and threshold calibration across languages
- Model explainability and fairness/drift monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function fuse(textModel, visualModel, audioModel) {
  // Attention-weighted fusion of modality scores
  const textScore = textModel.predict(textInput);
  const visualScore = visualModel.predict(videoFrames);
  const audioScore = audioModel.predict(audioStream);

  // Attention-based calibration head
  const weights = attentionLayer([textScore, visualScore, audioScore]);
  return weightedSum([textScore, visualScore, audioScore], weights);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests on-device multilingual NLP for real-time support escalation, balancing simplicity with practical constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **On-device inference**: Use TensorFlow Lite or ONNX Runtime Mobile for sub-100ms inference
- **Multilingual code-switch handling**: Character n-grams are language-agnostic; use Unicode normalization for emoji
- **Hybrid rule+ML approach**: Rules catch obvious cases (fast path), ML handles ambiguity
- **Latency targets**: 120ms p99 includes preprocessing + inference + postprocessing
- **Privacy**: On-device only, no data transmission, local differential privacy for any updates
- **Evaluation**: Offline metrics followed by staged rollout with careful monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// On-device scoring with TF-Lite
function scoreEscalation(messages, tfliteInterpreter) {
  const input = preprocessMessages(messages); // normalize, tokenize
  tfliteInterpreter.setInput(input);
  tfliteInterpreter.invoke();
  const output = tfliteInterpreter.output(0);
  return { score: output[0], threshold: 0.7 }; // escalate if above threshold
}

function checkRules(messages) {
  const urgentPatterns = [/\bhelp\b/i, /\burgent\b/i, /speak.*human/i];
  const urgentEmojis = ['🚨', '🙋', '😡', '😭'];
  const text = messages.join(' ');
  if (urgentPatterns.some(p => p.test(text)) || urgentEmojis.some(e => text.includes(e))) {
    return { escalate: true, confidence: 0.95, source: 'rule' };
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to drift or new escalation cues?
  - Use online learning with local batch updates, or periodic model refresh via federated learning
- How to measure impact on support workload and false positives?
  - Track: tickets deflected, false positive rate, escalation accuracy, user satisfaction scores

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates system design proficiency for cross-language NLP with strict privacy and real-time performance constraints. It also tests understanding of knowledge graph integration and disaster recovery mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- **Streaming multilingual NER**: Continuous entity extraction across 25 languages in real-time
- **Cross-language alias resolution**: Linking entities across different languages to a canonical form
- **Privacy-preserving per-tenant embeddings**: Isolated embedding spaces that prevent data leakage between tenants
- **Edge-friendly inference**: Lightweight models deployable close to data sources for minimal latency
- **Knowledge graph drift and rollback**: Tracking schema/behavior changes with the ability to revert safely

## Architecture Overview

```javascript
// Streaming pipeline architecture
async function processIncidentReport(text, tenant, metadata) {
  const normalized = await normalize(text);
  const entities = await runMultilingualNER(normalized, tenant);
  const linked = await resolveCrossLingualAliases(entities, sharedIndex);
  await updateKnowledgeGraph(linked, tenant, metadata);
  return { entities, aliases, confidence };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate cross-language alias accuracy across diverse language pairs?
- What rollback strategies would you implement for knowledge graph inconsistencies?
- How would you detect and handle model drift in production?
- What privacy guarantees do per-tenant embeddings provide against inference attacks?

